WEBVTT

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:04.902
A typical Open WebUI deployment includes a browser client, the Open WebUI application,

00:00:04.902 --> 00:00:09.990
backend database and configuration storage, one or more model runtimes or inference APIs,

00:00:09.990 --> 00:00:14.075
logs, uploaded files, user accounts, and sometimes a reverse proxy.

00:00:14.075 --> 00:00:15.940
The browser is where the user interacts.

00:00:15.940 --> 00:00:17.860
The application manages the workspace.

00:00:17.860 --> 00:00:19.680
The model backend performs inference.

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:23.110
Each layer has its own operational and security responsibilities.

00:00:23.110 --> 00:00:25.000
Prompts and responses move through several places.

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:26.920
A user writes a prompt in the browser.

00:00:26.920 --> 00:00:30.240
The application may add system instructions, conversation history,

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:33.120
selected model settings, or retrieved document context.

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:35.825
The request is then sent to a configured model endpoint.

00:00:35.825 --> 00:00:39.120
The response returns through the application and is displayed to the user,

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:42.195
often with conversation state stored for later use.

00:00:42.195 --> 00:00:46.300
Storage matters because Open WebUI can preserve configuration, users, conversations,

00:00:46.300 --> 00:00:49.680
uploaded content, knowledge collections, and application state.

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:52.270
Logs may capture operational events or errors.

00:00:52.270 --> 00:00:54.070
Backups may preserve the database and files.

00:00:54.070 --> 00:00:57.175
If storage is ephemeral, upgrades or restarts can lose state.

00:00:57.175 --> 00:01:01.570
If storage is persistent, it must be protected because it may contain sensitive prompts,

00:01:01.570 --> 00:01:03.850
outputs, files, and endpoint details.

00:01:03.850 --> 00:01:06.960
Architecture clarity helps operators troubleshoot and secure the system.

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:10.160
They should know where accounts are stored, how model endpoints are configured,

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:14.521
where files live, what data is logged, what is backed up, and whether access is local,

00:01:14.521 --> 00:01:15.760
LAN-based, or remote.

00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:19.760
A clean mental model prevents the interface from hiding important infrastructure choices.
